  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Empowering vulnerable young women</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Kigali, Rwanda, many children have been left highly vulnerable as a result of the 1994 Genocide and poverty in the country. RYico and its partner in Rwanda, Centre Marembo, work with orphaned and vulnerable children who fall through the cracks and do not receive the available support from the government and NGOs. These are children either born to sex workers, young sex workers, children who may have one or both parents in jail accused of participating in the genocide, and girls who take up informal domestic work rather than begging on the streets. The partners will work to ensure that these girls have good support systems and improved confidence and knowledge about how to protect themselves. They will offer short-term residential places for girls at high risk, and training and support to help girls gain the skills to better protect themselves. Through the project, the partners will also strengthen their skills and knowledge to engage with the community and policy makers to ensure that they act to protect these girls. As a result of the project, up to 2,720 girls will be in less risky positions and a safe space will exist for many more girls to access services and support.</narrative>
  </description>
